WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.348
An AI agent is a model in a loop that uses tools.

2
00:00:06.348 --> 00:00:09.327
But the model inside only produces text.

3
00:00:09.577 --> 00:00:13.978
It cannot open a network connection, or start kubectl by itself.

4
00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:17.927
The program around it, called the host, does that part.

5
00:00:17.927 --> 00:00:22.903
That is the agent you install, such as Claude Code, or Copilot in VS Code.

6
00:00:23.153 --> 00:00:29.069
On every call, the host sends the model your goal, the conversation so far, and a list of tools.

7
00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:35.651
Each tool is plain text too: a name, a short description, and the arguments it takes.

8
00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:37.802
The model replies with text.

9
00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:44.661
To use a tool, it writes a tool call in a fixed format: pods_list_in_namespace, with namespace hello.

10
00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:52.277
The host spots the tool call, asks for your approval if needed, and runs the tool, with the credentials you configured.

11
00:00:52.527 --> 00:00:57.654
Then it adds the result to the conversation, as text, and calls the model again.

12
00:00:57.904 --> 00:01:00.391
The model keeps no memory between calls.

13
00:01:00.391 --> 00:01:04.744
Each call carries the whole conversation again, tool results included.

14
00:01:05.494 --> 00:01:10.254
So it reads the events, then fetches the logs of the restarting container.

15
00:01:10.254 --> 00:01:12.441
One step at a time, the same loop.

16
00:01:12.691 --> 00:01:17.988
When a reply has no tool call in it, that reply is the answer, and the loop stops.

17
00:01:18.238 --> 00:01:25.477
One question can mean dozens of calls, and each call resends the whole conversation, so later steps cost more.

18
00:01:25.727 --> 00:01:32.304
So cap every run: a timeout on every CI job, and a spending limit on every API key.

19
00:01:33.354 --> 00:01:35.489
Where does the tool list come from?

20
00:01:35.489 --> 00:01:40.429
Without a standard, five agents and four systems need twenty pieces of glue code.

21
00:01:40.679 --> 00:01:47.820
With a shared protocol, each agent speaks it once, and each system gets one server: nine pieces.

22
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.465
That protocol is the Model Context Protocol, MCP.

23
00:01:52.515 --> 00:01:56.281
Inside the host, one MCP client talks to each server.

24
00:01:56.281 --> 00:02:01.041
A server wraps one system, such as a Kubernetes cluster, or GitHub.

25
00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:05.681
Each client asks its server for its tools, with a tools/list request.

26
00:02:05.681 --> 00:02:09.816
The host merges the answers into the one list it sends the model.

27
00:02:10.066 --> 00:02:15.705
When the model writes a tool call, the host passes it to the right client, which sends tools/call.

28
00:02:15.955 --> 00:02:22.489
The server does the real work, such as a request to the Kubernetes API, and returns the result.

29
00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:25.629
The messages are JSON-RPC 2.0.

30
00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:28.711
A local server talks over stdio.

31
00:02:28.711 --> 00:02:32.082
A remote one, over Streamable HTTP.

32
00:02:32.932 --> 00:02:35.046
So where do the guardrails sit?

33
00:02:35.046 --> 00:02:36.206
Not in the model.

34
00:02:36.206 --> 00:02:40.571
The MCP specification says the protocol cannot enforce security.

35
00:02:40.571 --> 00:02:46.164
It puts that job on the host, which must get your consent before it runs any tool.

36
00:02:46.814 --> 00:02:48.353
Next, the credentials.

37
00:02:48.353 --> 00:02:53.600
A tool can do only what its credentials allow, whatever the model asks for.

38
00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:02.834
In the lab, the Kubernetes server signs in as the ai-agent ServiceAccount: read-only in hello, with a token that expires in an hour.

39
00:03:03.084 --> 00:03:06.591
The server's read_only setting hides the tools that write.

40
00:03:06.591 --> 00:03:09.433
Its own docs call that a best-effort guardrail.

41
00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:13.576
The API server checks every call against the token's RBAC.

42
00:03:13.576 --> 00:03:16.059
A delete is refused there, Forbidden.

43
00:03:16.059 --> 00:03:17.938
That is the lock that holds.

44
00:03:18.688 --> 00:03:20.080
Then a person decides.

45
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.595
Keep the approval prompts on for commands that change things, and route changes through a pull request.

46
00:03:26.845 --> 00:03:34.467
And an agent that reads untrusted text, such as an issue or a log line, gets no write access and no secrets.

47
00:03:34.717 --> 00:03:36.124
The model only asks.

48
00:03:36.124 --> 00:03:41.963
The host runs the tool, MCP carries the call, and the token's rights set the limit.
